\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Regional flux and the reduced half-signature}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{The strip and reduced half-signatures of a regular \vocabk{domain-wall operator}{fdip-01B3} need not equal its mass-weighted regional flux, even for a scalar fiber on an even grid. With zero flux in both strips, the reduced half-signature can still change when the interior links change and the crossing hops stay fixed.}

\proof{
\p{Choose #{r} in \ref{fdip-01B7} also less than one quarter of the smaller of the two strip gains in \ref{fdip-01B8}. Each open restriction keeps its sign dimensions at both endpoints. Removing strip #{A} leaves the same hopping across the cuts and the same unreduced operator on #{B} at both endpoints. By \ref{fdip-01B5} the reduced half-signature on #{B} still differs by #{2}.}

\p{For the fractional regional flux, start at the positive-angle regular endpoint and multiply the transverse links on column #{m} by #{\ue^{\ui \alpha}}, as in \ref{fdip-01B4}. These new plaquettes are disjoint from the first two changed columns and their adjacent plaquettes. Choose #{0<\alpha<\pi /2} so small that #{|\ue^{\ui \alpha}-1|} is below the gains of the full operator and both open restrictions and that all angles remain in the chosen window. The same transverse-hop bound then keeps all three regular. The strip flux satisfies #{0<w_A=4\alpha /(2\pi)<1}. On this even-by-even grid the [bulk integer](fdip-019W) at mass #{1/3} is #{1}, since only the origin has negative shifted mass and its local orientation is positive. At mass #{5/2} the two corners with one coordinate #{\pi} also contribute, with negative orientation, giving #{1-2=-1}. Thus #{\kappa(M_A)w_A} and #{\kappa(M_B)w_B} are both fractional. For this minimal spin module on a scalar line the normalization #{(\dim S)/2} is #{1}. The open-strip and reduced half-signatures are integers, being half the difference of two sign dimensions with even sum. Neither can equal its corresponding fractional regional bulk term.}
}
